1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,279
Five teams. Everyone plays everyone. Could three of them improve their combined chance

2
00:00:05,279 --> 00:00:08,594
of taking the trophy by fixing just their own matches?

3
00:00:08,594 --> 00:00:13,320
Even the best fair rule must sometimes let them gain forty percentage points.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,426
Start with one result. An arrow from A to B means A beat B.

5
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:22,920
Each pair gets exactly one arrow. The complete picture records results, rather than the

6
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,004
order in which games were played.

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,309
A rule turns that picture into a champion.

8
00:00:28,309 --> 00:00:32,880
It may use a lottery. Here each team gets one fifth of the chance.

9
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:37,520
Five equal pieces make one whole probability, or a hundred percent.

10
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,637
We impose one clear fairness requirement.

11
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:45,376
If somebody beats everybody, that team must win the trophy for certain.

12
00:00:45,376 --> 00:00:51,316
This is called Condorcet consistency. When nobody is unbeaten, the rule has more freedom.

13
00:00:51,699 --> 00:00:57,198
Return to the circle. Every team beats the next two clockwise and loses to the other

14
00:00:57,198 --> 00:01:02,499
two. Each has two wins, so there is no unbeaten team to settle the choice for us.

15
00:01:02,899 --> 00:01:09,334
Now highlight A, D and E. Together they currently hold three fifths of the lottery.

16
00:01:09,334 --> 00:01:13,831
They are allowed to change only matches inside this group.

17
00:01:13,831 --> 00:01:17,707
Matches against B and C stay exactly as they were.

18
00:01:18,099 --> 00:01:24,512
A already beat B and C. If D and E now throw their games against A, those two arrows

19
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:31,077
reverse. Watch A become unbeaten. Fairness forces the rule to give A the entire trophy

20
00:01:31,077 --> 00:01:31,611
chance.

21
00:01:31,999 --> 00:01:34,395
The group goes from three fifths to one.

22
00:01:34,395 --> 00:01:37,748
Subtract the starting chance and the gain is two fifths.

23
00:01:37,748 --> 00:01:42,778
That means forty percentage points, measured for the group together, rather than for

24
00:01:42,778 --> 00:01:44,215
every member separately.

25
00:01:44,599 --> 00:01:48,966
Could an uneven lottery avoid this? Perhaps give A almost nothing.

26
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:54,655
The difficulty is that every team has its own version of this trick: join with the two

27
00:01:54,655 --> 00:01:57,367
teams that beat you, and become unbeaten.

28
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:00,625
Write those five groups underneath the teams.

29
00:02:00,625 --> 00:02:04,881
A appears in three groups. So does B, and so does every other team.

30
00:02:04,881 --> 00:02:10,534
Whatever probabilities the rule chooses, each probability is counted exactly three times.

31
00:02:10,933 --> 00:02:15,955
The team probabilities add to one, so the five starting group chances add to three.

32
00:02:15,955 --> 00:02:19,223
Each group can separately cheat its way to chance one.

33
00:02:19,223 --> 00:02:23,701
Their five possible gains therefore add to five minus three, which is two.

34
00:02:24,099 --> 00:02:28,549
Five numbers adding to two cannot all be smaller than two fifths.

35
00:02:28,549 --> 00:02:34,711
At least one group gains that much. This is the known lower-bound argument from Schneider,

36
00:02:34,711 --> 00:02:38,955
Schvartzman and Weinberg. No choice of a fair rule escapes it.

37
00:02:39,333 --> 00:02:42,016
But a lower bound is only half the answer.

38
00:02:42,016 --> 00:02:45,338
We also need a rule that never allows a bigger gain.

39
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:48,852
One nasty tournament anywhere could ruin the guarantee.

40
00:02:48,852 --> 00:02:52,941
So we must control the worst cheat across every possible result.

41
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:58,160
First remove duplicate work. Rename the five teams without changing any arrows.

42
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:03,292
The shape is the same. We can average over names without worsening the guarantee, so

43
00:03:03,292 --> 00:03:05,981
it is enough to store one lottery per shape.

44
00:03:06,366 --> 00:03:08,781
Five teams have twelve different shapes.

45
00:03:08,781 --> 00:03:12,886
Here are the twelve representatives in the actual small certificate.

46
00:03:12,886 --> 00:03:18,138
The rule is a lookup table: recognise the shape, read its fractions, then transfer them

47
00:03:18,138 --> 00:03:20,070
back to the original team names.

48
00:03:20,466 --> 00:03:26,761
Take this real row. The five chances are two fifths, three tenths, one fifth, one tenth,

49
00:03:26,761 --> 00:03:32,769
and zero. They add to one. The table contains different rows because different match

50
00:03:32,769 --> 00:03:35,130
results need different lotteries.

51
00:03:35,499 --> 00:03:38,287
For every allowed cheat, compare two rows.

52
00:03:38,287 --> 00:03:44,328
Add the cheating group's chances after the change, subtract its chances before, and require

53
00:03:44,328 --> 00:03:47,249
the answer to be at most our proposed limit.

54
00:03:47,249 --> 00:03:49,107
This creates one inequality.

55
00:03:49,499 --> 00:03:55,178
Do that for every group of two or three, and every way to change its internal matches.

56
00:03:55,178 --> 00:04:00,790
Groups of two matter too. The guarantee covers groups of at most three; checking only

57
00:04:00,790 --> 00:04:03,563
groups of exactly three would leave a gap.

58
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:08,495
Imagine each inequality cutting away forbidden choices of probabilities.

59
00:04:08,495 --> 00:04:11,663
A linear program searches the choices that remain.

60
00:04:11,663 --> 00:04:17,049
We ask whether all constraints can fit at two fifths, the limit already forced by the

61
00:04:17,049 --> 00:04:17,493
circle.

62
00:04:17,866 --> 00:04:23,662
The numerical solver suggests yes. But a decimal close to two fifths is not an exact

63
00:04:23,662 --> 00:04:28,768
guarantee. A tiny rounding error could put a constraint on the wrong side.

64
00:04:28,768 --> 00:04:32,218
The final rule must be a table of exact fractions.

65
00:04:32,599 --> 00:04:38,107
The discovery process rounds to fractions, repairs the constraints that must be equalities,

66
00:04:38,107 --> 00:04:43,554
and checks everything again. In the plain fair-rule certificates, only the known five-team

67
00:04:43,554 --> 00:04:46,519
circle configurations reach the worst-case limit.

68
00:04:46,899 --> 00:04:50,419
A separately written checker reads the resulting table.

69
00:04:50,419 --> 00:04:56,371
It verifies that every lottery sums to one, that unbeaten teams win, and that every permitted

70
00:04:56,371 --> 00:05:01,299
manipulation stays within the bound. It calculates with fractions throughout.

71
00:05:01,666 --> 00:05:05,963
At eight teams there are six thousand eight hundred and eighty shapes.

72
00:05:05,963 --> 00:05:11,979
The checker covers two million, eight hundred and eighty-nine thousand, six hundred manipulations.

73
00:05:11,979 --> 00:05:16,522
Its exact largest gain is two fifths. These are recorded completed checks.

74
00:05:16,899 --> 00:05:19,391
Why does checking shapes cover named teams?

75
00:05:19,391 --> 00:05:22,230
The lottery respects every symmetry of its shape.

76
00:05:22,230 --> 00:05:25,011
Relabelling therefore gives a consistent answer.

77
00:05:25,011 --> 00:05:30,051
For up to six teams, a second checker also expands all labelled tournaments and agrees.

78
00:05:30,433 --> 00:05:34,166
The same optimal values can satisfy two extra requirements.

79
00:05:34,166 --> 00:05:38,089
First, winning one more match never lowers that team's chance.

80
00:05:38,089 --> 00:05:43,467
This is monotonicity. We check it separately; it does not follow just from being hard

81
00:05:43,467 --> 00:05:44,353
to manipulate.

82
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:47,967
Second, winners come only from the top cycle.

83
00:05:47,967 --> 00:05:52,927
Picture a group whose members can reach every team by chains of wins.

84
00:05:52,927 --> 00:05:55,874
Teams outside that group get zero chance.

85
00:05:55,874 --> 00:05:59,037
Here a three-team cycle beats two outsiders.

86
00:05:59,433 --> 00:06:04,785
The eight-team certificate with both extra requirements passes the same manipulation

87
00:06:04,785 --> 00:06:09,754
checks, plus the extra tests. Across this package, fourteen certificates pass.

88
00:06:09,754 --> 00:06:15,106
The recovered eight-team result is an exact certificate, not an unfinished numerical

89
00:06:15,106 --> 00:06:15,489
solve.

90
00:06:15,866 --> 00:06:21,156
Now put the two halves together. The old argument says no rule can beat two fifths.

91
00:06:21,156 --> 00:06:25,681
The new tables achieve two fifths for five, six, seven and eight teams.

92
00:06:25,681 --> 00:06:29,186
Matching lower and upper bounds give the exact optimum.

93
00:06:29,566 --> 00:06:34,572
With three or four teams, the optimum for groups of at most three is one third.

94
00:06:34,572 --> 00:06:40,402
A separate seven-team certificate handles groups of at most four and reaches three sevenths.

95
00:06:40,402 --> 00:06:44,014
These are different finite cases, not one universal rule.

96
00:06:44,399 --> 00:06:48,565
For nine or more teams, this work gives no matching upper bound.

97
00:06:48,565 --> 00:06:52,339
A table for eight teams does not tell us how to extend it.

98
00:06:52,339 --> 00:06:58,131
If three-team collusion ever needs more than two fifths, the first failure needs at least

99
00:06:58,131 --> 00:06:58,847
nine teams.

100
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:02,319
Remember three ideas. Arrows describe results.

101
00:07:02,319 --> 00:07:06,143
A five-team circle forces an unavoidable cheating reward.

102
00:07:06,143 --> 00:07:10,102
Exact fraction tables meet that reward through eight teams.

103
00:07:10,102 --> 00:07:15,805
Earlier work already refuted the broader guess for some large groups; the small-group

104
00:07:15,805 --> 00:07:17,281
question remains open.

105
00:07:17,666 --> 00:07:22,831
The companion paper contains the full definitions, tables and independent checkers.

106
00:07:22,831 --> 00:07:27,374
This animation shows the lower-bound mechanism and the certificate logic.

107
00:07:27,374 --> 00:07:31,730
It does not replace the complete mathematical and computational proof.
